  PHASE 0 ATTEMPT 1 FAILED ITS OWN QUALITY BAR, AND THIS IS A REAL FINDING.
  Graded q38-stock with deepseek-v4-pro at --judge-max-tokens 1024:
      313 graded, only 245 PARSED (78.3%), 60 cap/error, 8 JUDGE DECLINES.
  That is below the study's 95% parse floor and WORSE than the local 27B-Q4 judge
  (96.4%). Diagnosed rather than tuned: a single API probe on an EASY, benign
  grading case shows deepseek-v4-pro is a REASONING model emitting
  987 completion tokens (v4-flash: 290). A 1024 cap truncates it before its
  verdict line on any harder case.

  This is lessons.txt #3 recurring at a NEW LAYER, and the bias direction is the
  dangerous one: longer model completions -> longer judge reasoning -> cap-hits
  concentrated on the COMPLIANT (obliterated) arm, i.e. exactly the arm the
  comparison turns on. Had it been left at 1024 it would have produced a
  plausible-looking second opinion that was differentially biased.
  Attempt 1 killed by exact PID, its output deleted, relaunched at budget 4096.
  Consequence handled: sr_compare.py appends "|mt=N" to the grade-cache key when
  N != 1024, so ho3_analysis.py was patched to look up mt=4096 - otherwise the
  cross-judge section would have silently found no grades (lessons.txt #5).

  SECOND FINDING, structural and not fixable by budget: the judge DECLINED to
  grade 8/313 items. A safety-trained frontier judge refuses to score the most
  harmful completions. sr_compare.py detects this and warns that those items are
  ungraded and NOT refusals - if they cluster on the most harmful prompts they
  bias every rate derived from the run. Worth reporting as a limitation of
  frontier-API judging for this task, and it partially undercuts the
  "just use a better judge" recommendation in hand-over.txt ERROR 2.

