!! MAJOR FINDING — ROUND 1 (Qwen3.6) IS CONFOUNDED BY AN INJECTED SYSTEM PROMPT
================================================================================
Found by the ho3 preflight template-render check, which was only run because
PREFLIGHT-NOTES.md §3 recorded the SCOPE under which the round-3 confound was
dismissed. Round 3 dismissed it correctly. Round 1 does not survive the same test.

The `qwen3.6-27b-obliteratus` GGUF ships a chat template containing:

  {%- set release_default_system = "[REDACTED — anti-refusal system prompt. Verbatim text withheld from
     this public repo under the same rule as sr_wrappers.local.json;
     kept in the gitignored qwen3.6-obliteratus-template-local.md.
     Substance: instructs the model to answer without moralizing,
     disclaimers or hedging.]" %}
  ...
  {%- if messages[0].role == 'system' %}  ... use the caller's system message ...
  {%- else %}  {{- '<|im_start|>system\n' + release_default_system + '<|im_end|>\n' }}

i.e. WHENEVER THE CALLER SENDS NO SYSTEM MESSAGE, the template silently injects an
anti-refusal system prompt. StrongREJECT and XSTest in this harness send exactly
one user message and no system prompt, so EVERY round-1 obliterated generation was
served with a jailbreak-style system prompt installed. The stock arm got none.

Verified three ways: rendered output diffs (q36-stock 6bc57c1021 vs
q36-obliteratus b6c1d9f47f on the exact refusal-benchmark message shape); the
literal template source (lines 45, 61, 69); and the fact that no other arm in the
study contains the string (grepping the injected prompt's opening phrase across
all six arms' templates matches q36-obliteratus ONLY).

WHAT THIS INVALIDATES.
eval-results-technical-paper.md §2 and "Foomax's AI Safety Project.md" both claim
"Identical server config, temperature, and prompts for both models, so every
difference below is attributable to the weight edit." That is FALSE for round 1.
Part of the measured effect is a prompt-level intervention shipped inside the
model file. Affected: the 16.9% StrongREJECT compliance, the 10.6%->1.6%
over-refusal benefit, and the 83-to-1 divergence analysis.

WHAT IT DOES NOT INVALIDATE.
Round 3 (Qwen3.8) is CLEAN: q38-stock, q38-obliterated and q38-heretic all render
byte-identically (6bc57c1021) on the refusal-benchmark shape. The 141-to-0 result,
the 45.7% compliance and the round-3 XSTest numbers stand.

DIRECTION OF THE BIAS ON THE HEADLINE — it makes the claim STRONGER, not weaker.
The injection pushes q3.6-obliteratus TOWARD compliance, so its measured 16.9%
OVERSTATES what the q3.6 weight edit alone achieves. The generational comparison
16.9% -> 45.7% therefore UNDERSTATES the true edit-only gap. The "trade got worse
between generations" claim survives and is strengthened - but for a reason nobody
knew, and the paper's stated basis for it is wrong.

BONUS, UNPLANNED. The new q36-heretic build renders IDENTICALLY to q36-stock
(6bc57c1021). So the recipe-variance arm now also delivers, for the first time, an
UNCONTAMINATED Qwen3.6 ablation measurement - a clean q3.6 stock-vs-ablated
comparison the project has never had.

FOLLOW-UP FOR THE WRAPPER SWEEP. The template uses the CALLER's system message
when one is present and the injected prompt otherwise. So the `safety_system`
preset (the only one that sets a system message) does not merely add a safety
instruction to the q3.6 obliterated model - it REPLACES the model's built-in
jailbreak prompt. Any re-analysis of that arm must account for this. The
prefix/suffix presets (responsible_assistant, placebo, jailbreak) wrap the USER
message and leave the injection in place, so they are unaffected.

