  local judge:     stock 190/200, obliterated 177/200  -> differential 13 items
  deepseek-v4-pro: stock 198/200, obliterated 192/200  -> differential  6 items
Coverage rises on both arms and the differential halves. Since the measured rates
agree to within 0.6 points between judges, the residual 6-item gap cannot be
carrying the finding.

DIAGNOSED, NOT SHRUGGED AT (hand-over3 required this if the gap persisted):
The "long completions exhaust the judge" hypothesis is REFUTED as a general
explanation. Median completion tokens, unparsed vs parsed:
  local / q38-obliterated ....... unparsed 174 vs parsed 220  (unparsed are SHORTER)
  deepseek / q38-stock .......... unparsed 189 vs parsed 155  (barely differs)
Only two of four cells show unparsed items being longer. Length is a contributor,
not the mechanism.

A DIFFERENT AND MORE INTERESTING MECHANISM SHOWED UP ON StrongREJECT.
There the LOCAL judge is near-perfect (312/313 and 311/313 - essentially no
differential) while DEEPSEEK is worse (308/313 and 300/313, differential 8), and
its unparsed items are NOT longer (median 235/274 vs parsed 241/296). The cause is
visible in sr_compare.py's own warning from attempt 1: the JUDGE DECLINED TO GRADE
8/313 items. A safety-trained frontier judge refuses to score exactly the most
harmful completions - and refuses more often on the arm that complies more.

CONSEQUENCE FOR hand-over.txt ERROR 2, WHICH I WROTE: "just use a better judge" is
too simple. A frontier judge is better on XSTest (coverage up, differential halved)
and WORSE on StrongREJECT (it declines to grade the load-bearing items). The right
configuration is judge-per-benchmark, and any frontier-judge run must report
judge-decline counts as a first-class number, not fold them into "unparsed".
